s not anybody will  stop us and that is what we have to do  [Applause]  um let me say a few words about the book  which I why I came here in the first  place look the first part of the book  deals with the campaign and those of you  are interested in politics will find it  I think interesting and we talked about  why I decided to run against the  sensible opposition of my wife Jane  who's sitting out here someplace  [Applause]  she being smarter than me said why in  god's name would you want to do this and  by the way one of the things that we  mentioned it in the book that she  worried about and i worried about is  that if we won what would happen the day  after  what would Wall Street do what would  corporate america do would they punish  the american people for having voted for  me probably what would the corporate  media have done think about it but i was  one of her worries and it was a  perceptive worry but unfortunately we  didn't have to worry about that  i wish we would have but we didn't  anyhow we talked about a campaign which  is ron gold flawed mentioned we started  off with virtually nothing i know  something about politics in my own state  of vermont and by the way in the summer  when it gets a little hot here come  visit us you'll find it a bit cooler  where a very beautiful state we're a  small state we have about six hundred  and twenty five thousand people and i  know how to campaign in my own state in  my last election i got 71 percent of the  vote so we know how to do that pretty  well but running for US Senate in  vermont is very different and running  for president of the united states and  we've started off knowing virtually  nothing about how to do that and we  talked about what we learned and how we  progress and what i did you know very  often candidates say when asked if  they're going to run they say well you  know they're talking to people and so  actually that was the case with me what  we did is we went around the country and  i wanted to hear what people said did  people really want a candidacy and a  candidate who was prepared to take on  the economic establishment the political  establishment did they really want that  and as I came around went around the  country again as somebody who was not  very well known at that point uh what I  found in meetings all over the country  hundreds of people were coming out and  yes they were sick and tired of  establishment politics and establishment  economics and that's what I pick up  around the country and finally we  decided you know we would do  and we went forward our and then what we  saw is as we made when you run a  campaigners you can think about it okay  what is what do you do what and we  decided for a number of reasons  that we would make rallies meetings like  this a little bit larger than this often  the the cornerstone of the campaign  because for a variety of reasons when  you you know a lot of politicians use  people like yourselves right now it's  kind of a backdrop because what's  important is the TV cameras say what I'm  saying  you're just the backdrop for the TV  cameras but I don't like that I like  talking to people I like being here I  like looking you in the eye and I like  answering and I like answering questions  and what we found is we run around the  country that more and more people were  coming out and it is a bit of a  mind-blowing experiencing experience  walking out in an arena in Portland  Oregon where the Trail Blazers play the  NBA team and looking at at 28,000 people  and I think there are people outside so  the other thing that we try to do we  understood politically if you run in the  primaries we have a pretty crazy system  in that everybody in politics knows that  the first states are very very important  in the first two states while Iowa first  and then a week later New Hampshire so  what we understood is what every  politician runs for president in the  sense if you do poorly in those states  you kind of get p